                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "487356cb-011e-39bb-b78e-51b2beaa4231",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to covering or putting on.",
                            "Of a person or thing: to take on (a different form); to adopt, to assume."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "also",
                            "figuratively",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1269ac0d-5b79-4195-9854-9fda02915414",
                                "sense_id": "487356cb-011e-39bb-b78e-51b2beaa4231",
                                "text": "Infinite ſhapes of creatures there [in the Garden of Eden] are bred, / And vncouth formes, vvhich none yet euer knevv, / […] / Some fitt for reaſonable ſovvles t’indew, / Some made for beaſts, ſome made for birds to vveare, […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f872aef8-3e85-4fbc-9da8-6f217617d752",
                                "sense_id": "487356cb-011e-39bb-b78e-51b2beaa4231",
                                "text": "Pallas [Athena], forth-vvith, an old-vviues ſhape indues; / Her hair all vvhite; her lims, appearing vveake, / A ſtaffe ſupports: vvho thus began to ſpeake."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3eef6534-3755-462b-81d3-b06db3710271",
                                "sense_id": "487356cb-011e-39bb-b78e-51b2beaa4231",
                                "text": "Could I outwear my present state of woe / With one brief winter, and indue i' the spring / Hues of fresh youth, and mightily outgrow / That wan dark coil of faded suffering— […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "60a3a25f-f6e0-49ac-acaa-ba6d2320a2bb",
                                "sense_id": "487356cb-011e-39bb-b78e-51b2beaa4231",
                                "text": "My transport of the afternoon, and the matter of physical contrast, made me endue the tactile apparatus of another man, any man but me, and imagine the beauty of Zip in his caressing arms."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "054d9cb2-3c9f-3c2f-aaab-b0b8e04e93b1",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to covering or putting on.",
                            "To put on (a piece of clothing, etc.); to wear; also (followed by with), to clothe (someone) with something."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "also",
                            "archaic",
                            "figuratively",
                            "literary",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f04b9899-5b10-4473-8b3d-84263a3ec47a",
                                "sense_id": "054d9cb2-3c9f-3c2f-aaab-b0b8e04e93b1",
                                "text": "Indu'd vvith Robes of various Hevv ſhe [Iris] flies, / And flying dravvs an Arch, (a ſegment of the Skies:) / Then leaves her bending Bovv, and from the ſteep / Deſcends to ſearch the ſilent Houſe of Sleep."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9daf7c91-3bfc-4f7b-98fa-e309924c7ebc",
                                "sense_id": "054d9cb2-3c9f-3c2f-aaab-b0b8e04e93b1",
                                "text": "[T]he Baron, with the help of Mr Saunderson, had indued a pair of jack[-]boots of large dimension, and now invited our hero to follow him as he stalked clattering down the ample staircase, […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "116a96a0-331e-4475-9b99-63c6ff1986ea",
                                "sense_id": "054d9cb2-3c9f-3c2f-aaab-b0b8e04e93b1",
                                "text": "But before the chiefs and captains rode to their several posts, they assembled around William, whom Fitzosborne had called betimes, and who had not yet endued his heavy mail, that all men might see suspended from his throat certain relics chosen out of those on which Harold had pledged his fatal oath."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6e5b0d4a-c8c8-4325-9349-fb2a434e24e2",
                                "sense_id": "054d9cb2-3c9f-3c2f-aaab-b0b8e04e93b1",
                                "text": "Governor Bellingham, in a loose gown and easy cap,—such as elderly gentlemen loved to indue themselves with, in their domestic privacy,—walked foremost, and appeared to be showing off his estate, and expatiating on his projected improvements."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a1c600af-7f07-44ce-82ef-fa41391393d7",
                                "sense_id": "054d9cb2-3c9f-3c2f-aaab-b0b8e04e93b1",
                                "text": "[…] John Fry's sad-coloured Sunday hat was indued with a plume of marish-weed."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0894c64f-52ff-4703-b0e7-7469bd79bc46",
                                "sense_id": "054d9cb2-3c9f-3c2f-aaab-b0b8e04e93b1",
                                "text": "Judaea greeted its monarch. He was to ascend to the immemorial sacring place of millennia of kings, there to be endued with the robe and crown of rule."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cb88f6ca-1944-39e3-8794-b331edc6a7f9",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to covering or putting on.",
                            "To put (something) on top of a thing; to cover, to overlay."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "rare",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5eadb4d1-3b80-4dfa-a205-ce984678c077",
                                "sense_id": "cb88f6ca-1944-39e3-8794-b331edc6a7f9",
                                "text": "[The altar in St. Peter's Basilica] of St. Veronica made by Fra[ncesco] Mochi, has over it in the Reliquary, where they shew'd us the miraculous Sudarium indued wᵗʰ the picture of oʳ Saviour [Jesus]'s face, […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to giving some quality or thing.",
                            "Followed by with: to invest (someone or something) with a certain power, quality, etc."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "literary",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fe8f37ea-1943-49d0-84ec-9a56be747c0c",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "[L]earning endueth mens mindes vvith a true ſence of the frailtie of their perſons, the caſualtie of their fortunes, and the dignitie of their ſoule and vocation; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1d7d3f6f-4b34-42a8-86a0-56d7afe1334f",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "And behold, I [Jesus] ſend the promiſe of my Father vpon you: but tarie ye in the citie of Hieruſalem, vntill ye be indued with power from on high."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "739e0c9d-5496-4d4f-8176-90a660e47479",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "VVhen I called upon thee, thou heardeſt me: and enduedſt my ſoul vvith much ſtrength."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1898df7f-a42c-4a63-93fa-516a790f196d",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "That the Sun, Moone, and Stars are living creatures, endued vvith ſoule and life, ſeemes an innocent error, and a harmeleſſe digreſſion from truth; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6190683f-90f3-409b-b491-cbe08150dba3",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "Some aſcribe to the ſanctity of this Ioſeph [of Arimathea], the yearly budding of the Havv-thorn near Glaſſenbury, on Chriſtmas day, no leſſe then an annual Miracle. […] Hovvever, there is no neceſſity, that this ſhould be imputed to the Holineſs of Arimathean Ioſeph. For there is (as is credibly ſaid) an Oake in Nevv-Foreſt, nigh Lindhurſt, in Hantſhire, vvhich is indued vvith the ſame quality, putting forth leaves about the ſame time; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "31c6c26c-ed35-4838-ae1a-c32e5fe79044",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "God made man for himſelf in his ovvn image; vvith Reaſon and freevvill: endued vvith vviſdom and holineſs; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "792e5a19-cbf5-4360-84d6-287cfcbd78c0",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "Thus vvas th' accompliſh'd Squire endu'd / VVith gifts and knovvledge, per'lous ſhrevvd."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "954f9cea-8a53-47b4-b28c-e01c092d823b",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "[T]he apples ſeemed hardly to be fermented, but vvere endued vvith a moſt pleaſant taſte."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "adbd2143-3c91-434b-820c-ecbf1224d887",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "[…] Kings, though not indued vvith the Supream Povver, are not by Vertue of that Sovereign and Kingly Right of theirs Lords over the people, nor ought to Govern them alone; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "61da0e7c-c828-46f4-880b-a2ad2e01e599",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "[W]ere our Eyes turned into the Nature of Microſcopes, […] our Sight vvou'd be endued vvith a far greater Sharpneſs and Penetration than it novv hath."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4586472c-a0a0-4fb3-b312-495e3f58bfdb",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "A being whom I myself had formed, and endued with life, had met me at midnight among the precipices of an inaccessible mountain."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f8eede7b-898c-4ad7-a9dd-f3e5daaf26c5",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "Of simple men and truest / Faces of girl and boy; / The souls whom thou enduest / With gentle peace and joy."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "da2bcf5c-9efc-400b-a4b9-662127879b78",
                                "sense_id": "b8c3c74f-c0fa-3f2e-919d-a5b1262dfa29",
                                "text": "But after dissension / Had ended, in France, and you were endued / With your former privilege, how did you show your gratitude?"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2a474e94-6528-32d6-a224-ed82d8ea6d5c",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to giving some quality or thing.",
                            "Of a quality, etc.: to be inherent in (something)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c148f401-b642-4257-882e-8d93930ce010",
                                "sense_id": "2a474e94-6528-32d6-a224-ed82d8ea6d5c",
                                "text": "Perhaps my ſemblance might deceive the truth, / That I to manhood am arriv'd ſo near, / And invvard ripenes doth much leſs appear / That ſom more timely-happy ſpirits indu'th."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "20f6b1ff-8520-4ce6-b3e0-d0a6e71c668c",
                                "sense_id": "2a474e94-6528-32d6-a224-ed82d8ea6d5c",
                                "text": "Lion vvhom the footſteps of the fox purſue, / VVhoſe ſouls deceit and vanity endue."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "23e798a3-8106-3d43-a76b-4fc287a40ee8",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to giving some quality or thing.",
                            "To supply (someone) with a thing."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d98928fb-ec78-4890-9b25-689c538d0fe0",
                                "sense_id": "23e798a3-8106-3d43-a76b-4fc287a40ee8",
                                "text": "Some reaſons of this double Coronation / I haue poſſeſt you vvithm, and thinke them ſtrong, / And more, more ſtrong, then leſſer is my feare / I ſhall indue you vvith: […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a89e5d61-14b4-4331-bf65-df67b7dfba87",
                                "sense_id": "23e798a3-8106-3d43-a76b-4fc287a40ee8",
                                "text": "[T]he Tribunes endue you vvith the Peoples Voyce, […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4bac6f83-7caa-3300-a238-2422dfd73068",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to giving some quality or thing.",
                            "Synonym of endow (“to invest (a person, group of people, or institution) with property”)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a35a6c2c-5182-4327-9046-4a1b6400b5a5",
                                "sense_id": "4bac6f83-7caa-3300-a238-2422dfd73068",
                                "text": "[H]e [Henry V] would be content to take in mariage the Lady Katheryn [Catherine of Valois] daughter vnto the French king [Charles VI of France], and to endue her with all the Duchye and countryes before reherſed."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "60ae562d-11cf-4767-97d0-db4a90738417",
                                "sense_id": "4bac6f83-7caa-3300-a238-2422dfd73068",
                                "text": "And Leah ſaid, God hath endued me with a good dowry: Now will my huſband dwel with me, because I haue borne him ſixe ſonnes: and ſhee called his name Zebulun."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f86e9e59-cf76-3059-af17-be518e17ba98",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to giving some quality or thing.",
                            "Synonym of bestow (“to impart (something) gratuitously; to grant”)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "rare",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "281da985-496d-32e9-99cd-14cf58134379",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to directing or leading.",
                            "To raise or rear (someone); to bring up; also, to educate or instruct (someone)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "945dc6be-89e2-3753-8541-50b7896973ba",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to directing or leading.",
                            "To bring (something) to a certain condition."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "rare",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "85859c0d-00e2-4fc5-85ce-68d195cdd365",
                                "sense_id": "945dc6be-89e2-3753-8541-50b7896973ba",
                                "text": "[L]et our finger ake, / And it endues our other healthfull members, / Euen to that ſence of paine; […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cd92914e-dc19-3448-ba96-b50b16a1d307",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to taking in.",
                            "Of a hawk: originally, to pass (food in the crop or gizzard) into the stomach; later, to digest (food)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0fa18553-05bc-48ac-b5e6-371ad2df6b8f",
                                "sense_id": "cd92914e-dc19-3448-ba96-b50b16a1d307",
                                "text": "Your gorge not endewed / Without a capon stewed"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "87300164-ca02-4e73-93e9-4fc0c22e935b",
                                "sense_id": "cd92914e-dc19-3448-ba96-b50b16a1d307",
                                "text": "I Mute [defecate] as a hauke dothe whan ſhe hath endued her gorge."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "78ae4ac5-f30e-499b-8b2c-62383239779b",
                                "sense_id": "cd92914e-dc19-3448-ba96-b50b16a1d307",
                                "text": "Cattes fleſhe is vnholeſome, and harde to be indewed, and breedeth perillous wormes, and ſtoppeth a Hawke in the gorge, and marreth hir winde."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "37398781-fcbb-3d6d-ba9d-c53579c36726",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to taking in.",
                            "Of a person or animal: to digest (food)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly",
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e54c5515-30ae-4ab4-8238-69099b13daf9",
                                "sense_id": "37398781-fcbb-3d6d-ba9d-c53579c36726",
                                "text": "Her mete was very crude, / She had not wel endude; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "458ba2e7-91c4-4bfa-af57-cfdfa7c45685",
                                "sense_id": "37398781-fcbb-3d6d-ba9d-c53579c36726",
                                "text": "All, (not only we) wait upon God; and he gives them their meat, though not our meat, (the word and the sacraments) yet their meat, such as they are able to digest and endue."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0d74a24d-81e8-4260-842a-c1c1b929a9ce",
                                "sense_id": "37398781-fcbb-3d6d-ba9d-c53579c36726",
                                "text": "The Birds were large, fine, and neat accordingly; looking as like the Men in my Country, as one Pea do's like another; for they eat and drank like Men, muted like Men, endued or digested like Men, farted like Men, but stunk like Devils, […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "548ab353-6ac1-39eb-bda6-f83b793f79cc",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to taking in.",
                            "To take on; to absorb."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "figuratively",
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "45ad85bd-58d1-48d4-bf47-cdb0a4388006",
                                "sense_id": "548ab353-6ac1-39eb-bda6-f83b793f79cc",
                                "text": "He did her ſeruice devvtifull, and ſevvd / At hand vvith humble pride, and pleaſing guile, / So cloſely yet, that none but ſhe it vevved, / VVho vvell perceiued all, and all indevvd."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "fefe39ce-0cb4-3098-a689-478388975f48",
                        "entry_id": "3f230a53-774a-345f-8905-49216a4db797",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Senses relating to taking in.",
                            "Of food: to be digested."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "intransitive",
                            "obsolete",
                            "rare"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
